$ Elements Supported
$ Ga  Ge  As  Se  Br  Kr 
$ In  Sn  Sb  Te  I   Xe 
$ Tl  Pb  Bi  Po  At  Rn
$ Uut Uuq Uup Uuh Uus Uuo
$
$  REFERENCE
$
$ 4p-6p: K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (1998)  99:366;
$          addendum  Theor. Chem. Acc. (2002) 108:365;
$          revision  Theor. Chem. Acc. (2006) 115:441
$    7p: K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (2012) 131:1172
$ Available from the Dirac web site, http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$
$


$ 
$ Basis set library file automatically generatered by basis_set.py
$ 
$ invocation: basis_set.py --file=../basis/reference_edited/4p_qz_archive --print_polarization --print_scfbas --uncontracted --equal_exponent_threshold=0.05 --polarization_type=valence correlating,core correlating,diffuse --output=dyall.4p.qz.bas
$ 
$ These are the sets of functions selected from the TCA archive (apart from SCF):
$  ['valence correlating', 'core correlating', 'diffuse']
$ 
$ dyall.4p.qz.bas
$
$ el contained in the file 
$ Ga As Kr Ge Br Se
$ 
$ 4p-Block Quadruple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ =========================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 18 Nov 2009
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (2006) 115:441. Basis sets available from the Dirac web site, 
$ http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the double-zeta basis sets described in the above article.  
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * Optimization of the s, p, and d sets separately, on the total energy of the
$   configuration average of the s^2 p^n configuration, to yield a 30s 21p 12d
$   set.
$ * Replacement of the outermost 2 SCF d exponents with the valence correlating 3d
$   exponent set and reoptimization of the other 10 d exponents, with the
$   correlating d exponents fixed
$ 
$ The resulting primitive basis set is a 30s 21p 13d set. In all the following
$ descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF exponent set. 
$ 
$ 
$ The correlating exponents were generated as follows
$ 
$ * A 3d2f1g exponent set for 4s4p correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
$   on the ground configuration, with 3 s and 3 p exponents from the SCF set
$   included in the correlating space and held fixed.
$ * A 3f2g1h exponent set for 3d correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
$   on the ground configuration, with double excitations from the 3d shell into
$   the f shell coupled to J=0.
$ 
$ The diffuse exponent sets were generated as follows.
$ 
$ * An s exponent and a p exponent were optimized in SCF calculations on the
$   negative ion for Ga - Br, with the remaining s and p exponents fixed
$ * A dfg exponent set was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the negative 
$   ion for Ga - Br, with 3 s and 3 p exponents from the SCF set, the correlating
$   3d2f1g set, and the diffuse sp set included in the correlating space, i.e. a 
$   4s4p4d3f2g correlating space.
$ * The s and p exponents were extrapolated in a geometric sequence from Se and Br
$   to obtain exponents for Kr. The d, f, and g exponents for Kr were determined by
$   multiplying the smallest correlating exponent for Kr by the ratio of the diffuse
$   exponent to the smallest correlating exponent for Br.
$ 
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
$    of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
$    component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
$    symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
$    those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
$    The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
$    j=l+1/2.
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of diffuse s, p, d, f, and g functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of valence correlating functions.
$ 
$ 5. Exponents of core correlating functions.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive 
$ functions. These basis sets are described below.
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ 1. Valence flexibility (equivalent to cc-pvqz). To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
$    1st, 2nd, and 4th s primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 3rd, and 4th p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd, and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set (correlating 3d)
$    valence correlating 2f1g functions.
$ 
$    This it the recommended valence contraction, because it provides the best balance 
$    between valence flexibility and valence correlation. There is less than 100 uEh
$    spread in the energies for all possible choices of 3 of the outermost 4 p functions.
$ 
$    This basis set has a less-than-optimal correlation energy. Losses in the s set 
$    relative to the best correlation energy are up to 64 uEh for Kr, and in the p set 
$    vary from 35 uEh for Ga to 923 uEh for Kr. The optimum p set is 3rd, 4th, 5th, 
$    which does not provide the best  valence flexibility and correlates the second
$    innermost antinode of the 4p function. The losses in the s set are insignificant, 
$    but the losses in the p set might be significant in an extrapolation. However, 
$    extrapolation is used to obtain accurate results, and without core correlation, 
$    the extrapolation might not be useful. The addition of the core correlating set 
$    (set 2 below) adds the functions that provide the optimum p correlation. 
$ 
$    An alternative for the p set is 1st, 2nd, 4th, which has smaller losses but
$    does not provide the optimum p correlation when the core set is added.
$ 
$    A contraction designed for optimum valence correlation is given in basis set 3.
$ 
$    (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence)
$ 
$ 2. Core-valence flexibility. To basis set 1, add the following:
$    5th, 6th, and 7th s primitives from the SCF set
$    5th, 6th, and 7th p primitives from the SCF set
$    4th, 6th, and 7th d primitives from the SCF set
$    core correlating 3f2g1h functions.
$ 
$    (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence + 3s3p3d3f2g1h core)
$ 
$ 3. Valence correlation. To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
$    3rd, 4th, and 5th s primitives from the SCF set
$    3rd, 4th, and 5th p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd, and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set (correlating 3d)
$    valence correlating 2f1g functions.
$ 
$    Note: The outermost s and p primitives are not uncontracted in this basis set, 
$    which could result in less-than-optimal valence flexibility, and might degrade
$    the quality of molecular calculations. The s set is not quite optimal for Ge,
$    Ga and As, but the errors are about 10 uEh. The optimal s set for these elements
$    is 1st, 2nd, and 4th. 
$ 
$    (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence)
$ 
$ 4. Core-valence correlation. To basis set 3, add the following:
$    6th, 7th, and 8th s primitives from the SCF set
$    6th, 7th, and 8th p primitives from the SCF set
$    4th, 6th, and 7th d primitives from the SCF set
$    core correlating 3f2g1h functions.
$ 
$    (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence + 3s3p3d3f2g1h core)
$ 
$ 5. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p, d, f, and g functions to any of the above
$    basis sets.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

