$ 
$ Basis set library file automatically generatered by basis_set.py
$ 
$ invocation: basis_set.py --file=../basis/reference_edited/6p_qz_archive --print_polarization --print_scfbas --uncontracted --equal_exponent_threshold=0.05 --polarization_type=valence correlating,core correlating,diffuse --output=dyall.6p.qz.bas
$ 
$ These are the sets of functions selected from the TCA archive (apart from SCF):
$  ['valence correlating', 'core correlating', 'diffuse']
$ 
$ dyall.6p.qz.bas
$
$ el contained in the file 
$ Bi Pb Tl At Rn Po
$ 
$ 6p-Block Quadruple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ =========================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 13 Nov 2009
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (2006) 115:441. Basis sets available from the Dirac web site, 
$ http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the double-zeta basis sets described in the above article.  
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * Optimization of the s, p, d, and f sets separately, on the total energy of the
$   configuration average of the s^2 p^n configuration, to yield a 34s 30p 20d 12f
$   set.
$ * Replacement of the outermost 2 SCF d exponents with the valence correlating 3d
$   exponent set and reoptimization of the other 18 d exponents, with the
$   correlating d exponents fixed
$ * Addition of the valence correlating 2f set to the SCF set, and reoptimization
$   of the other 12 f exponents with the correlating f exponents fixed.
$ 
$ The resulting primitive basis set is a 34s 30p 21d 14f set. In all the following
$ descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF exponent set. 
$ 
$ 
$ The correlating exponents were generated as follows
$ 
$ * A 3d2f1g exponent set for 6s6p correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
$   on the ground configuration, with 3 s and 3 p exponents from the SCF set
$   included in the correlating space and held fixed. (These are the 1st and 3rd
$   s and p exponents, determined to be the optimal choice for valence correlation).
$ * A 3f2g1h exponent set for 5d correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
$   on the ground configuration, with double excitations from the 5d shell into
$   the f shell coupled to J=0.
$ 
$ The diffuse exponent sets were generated as follows.
$ 
$ * An s exponent and a p exponent were optimized in SCF calculations on the
$   negative ion for Tl - At, with the remaining s and p exponents fixed
$ * A dfg exponent set was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the negative 
$   ion for Tl - At, with 3 s and 3 p exponents from the SCF set, the correlating
$   3d2f1g set, and the diffuse sp set included in the correlating space, i.e. a 
$   4s4p4d3f2g correlating space.
$ * The s and p exponents were extrapolated in a geometric sequence from Po and At
$   to obtain exponents for Rn. The d, f, and g exponents for Rn were determined by
$   multiplying the smallest correlating exponent for Rn by the ratio of the diffuse
$   exponent to the smallest correlating exponent for At.
$ 
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in
$    order of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the
$    small component functions. Both components are labelled with the large
$    component symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component
$    are those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component. 
$    The spin-orbit components are labeled with "-" for j=l-1/2 and "+" for j=l+1/2.   
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of diffuse s, p, d, f, and g functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of valence correlating functions.
$ 
$ 5. Exponents of core correlating functions.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive 
$ functions. These basis sets are described below. The p set is difficult to specify
$ for all elements and include the outermost functions, so various contraction strategies
$ are suggested. 
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ 1. Valence flexibility (equivalent to cc-pvqz). To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
$    1st, 2nd, and 4th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    3rd, 4th, and 5th p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st, 2nd, and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set (correlating 3d)
$    1st and 2nd f primitives from the SCF set (correlating 2f)
$    valence correlating 1g function.
$ 
$    Note: the outermost p primitives are not uncontracted, which could be a problem 
$    for molecular calculations. An alternative is to use the 1st, 4th, and 5th, with
$    a loss of 130 - 290 uEh, or the 2nd, 3rd, and 5th p primitives, with a loss of
$    460 - 580 uEh. Another alternative is to use the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th, with a loss
$    of 90 - 780 uEh; with this alternative the loss is made up when the core
$    correlating functions are added. 
$ 
$    (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence)
$ 
$ 2. Core-valence flexibility. To basis set 1, add the following:
$    5th, 6th, and 8th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    5th, 6th, and 8th p primitives from the SCF set
$    5th, 6th, and 7th d primitives from the SCF set
$    3rd, 4th, and 5th f primitives from the SCF set
$    core correlating 2g1h functions.
$ 
$    Note: the optimum p primitives are 4th, 5th, and 6th for Tl and Pb, and
$    5th, 6th, and 8th for Bi - Rn. However, the 4th and 5th are already in
$    the valence set. An alternative is to change the valence basis, and use
$    the 1st, 3rd, 4th, 5th, 6th, and 8th for the combined set. This covers
$    the optimal set for both valence and core correlation, and includes the
$    outermost function for valence flexibility.
$ 
$    An alternative core-valence p set is to use the 1st, 3rd, 4th, 5th, 6th, 
$    and 8th p primitives. This set covers the optimum core and valence
$    correlation and allows better for valence flexibility. 
$ 
$    (HF + 3s3p3d2f1g valence + 3s3p3d3f2g1h core)
$ 
$ 3. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p, d, f and g functions to any of the above
$    basis sets.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

